WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.703
An LLM answers by writing words.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:04.963
Jev never writes.

3
00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:06.946
It picks one of your options.

4
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.774
And it tells you how sure it is.

5
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.593
Every request is one of three shapes.

6
00:00:13.593 --> 00:00:16.257
A choice, a score, or a single
probability.

7
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.534
That number decides what you automate.

8
00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:21.298
Above your threshold it ships.

9
00:00:21.298 --> 00:00:22.768
Below it, a person looks.

10
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.089
Calibrated means this.

11
00:00:25.089 --> 00:00:29.013
Say seventy percent, and be right about
seventy times in a hundred.

12
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.311
The same answers, under a dishonest
number.

13
00:00:32.311 --> 00:00:33.713
That gap is the problem.

14
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.075
So why report a number that makes it look
unsure?

15
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.875
Score it on a straight line, and the
cheapest answer is always total certainty.

16
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.537
Score it on this curve instead, and the
cheapest answer is exactly what it really believes.

17
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.075
That is what RLCD is named after.

18
00:00:53.075 --> 00:00:54.798
Not constrained decoding.

19
00:00:54.798 --> 00:00:56.245
Calibrated decisions.

20
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.316
TypeSafe publishes the contract.

21
00:00:59.316 --> 00:01:02.780
The loss, the data, and any calibration
number are not.

22
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.471
It decides, and it says how sure.
